auth {
  mode: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

vars:pre-request {
}

docs {
  # agent-1o1-ai API

  Internal V1 API (bearer/Passport auth) plus the Public V1 API (API-key auth).

  ## Response envelope

  Every Internal V1 response is wrapped: `{ success, statusCode, message, data }`
  on success, `{ success, statusCode, message, errors? }` on failure (`errors`
  present for 422 validation failures). Actual payloads always live under
  `data` — e.g. login's tokens are at `data.tokens`, not top-level. Endpoints
  that return no body (delete, logout, revoke) still respond `204 No Content`
  with no envelope at all.

  ## Auth flow

  1. Run `auth/Register` or `auth/Login`. Their post-response script stores
     `accessToken` as a collection variable. The refresh token is set as an
     httpOnly `refresh_token` cookie, so keep Bruno's cookie jar on for
     `Auth/Refresh Token`.
  2. Every other Internal V1 request inherits collection-level bearer auth
     and automatically uses `{{accessToken}}`.
  3. Requests under `workspaces/{workspace}/...` also need a `workspaceId`
     variable set (see the `local` environment) — set it after creating or
     listing a workspace.
  4. Public V1 requests (`public/...`) use a workspace API key instead —
     set `{{apiKey}}` from `workspaces/api-keys/Create Api Key`.
  5. The webhook endpoints under `Webhooks` use neither: the token in the
     URL is the whole auth story. See that folder's requests.

  ## Resource ID variables

  Most folders' `Create` requests store the new record's ID in a collection
  variable, so the rest of that folder works without hand-editing paths:
  `workflowId`, `versionId`, `runId`, `triggerId`, `triggerToken`,
  `builderSessionId`, `agentId`, `agentSessionId`, `toolBindingId`,
  `skillId`, `skillReferenceId`, `skillScriptId`, `folderId`, `tagId`,
  `creditPackId`, `secretId`, `workflowTemplateId`, `agentTemplateId`,
  `templateCollectionId`, `templateCollectionItemId`, `evalSuiteId`,
  `evalCaseId`, `evalRunId`, and `planId` (set by `Billing/Plans - List`).
  The `Referrals` and `Admin` folders store `referralCode`,
  `referralVisitorId`, `referralProgramId`, `referralRuleId`,
  `referralCodeId`, `referralId`, `referralRewardId` and `blockedDomainId`
  from their `Get`/`List`/`Create` requests.
  `Assistant`, `Pages`, `Ai`, `Knowledge Base`, `Library` and the newer
  `Agents`/`Skills`/`Workflows` requests do the same with their own
  prefixed variables (`assistantSessionId`, `pageId`, `pageToken`,
  `aiProviderCredentialId`, `modelCatalogId`, `knowledgeSourceId`,
  `skillSourceId`, `publishRequestId`, `agentActionId`, `agentPlanId`, …).
  Every variable used anywhere in the collection is listed, empty, in the
  environments.
  Running a folder top to bottom fills them in as it goes.

  A few have no `Create` to populate them and must be set by hand from a
  response body: `approvalId` (from a run parked on an approval node),
  `waitToken` (from the callback URL a Wait node hands out),
  `workflowNodeId` (a node's ID from a workflow's graph — not the same
  thing as `nodeId`, which is a node *catalog* entry), and the `nodeRun`
  path params under `Runs` and `Public`.

  `Artifacts - Preview` and `Webhooks/Stripe Webhook` can't be run by
  filling in a variable at all — the first needs a signed URL taken whole
  from an artifact response, the second a Stripe-signed payload. Both say
  so in their own docs.

  ## Suggested order

  `Auth` → `Workspaces` (create one, then an API key) → `Workflows` →
  `Workflow Builder` (draft a graph) → `Workflows/Versions - Publish` →
  `Runs`. `Agents`, `Skills`, `Secrets`, and `Triggers` are independent of
  that chain apart from needing `workspaceId` — though attaching a workflow
  tool or pointing a trigger at a workflow needs `workflowId` too.

  `SuperAdmin` is a separate API with its own accounts and token
  (`superAdminToken`): see that folder's docs for how to sign in. It needs
  `SUPER_ADMIN_ENABLED=true`.

  `Templates` needs an existing workflow or agent before its
  `Save From ...` requests do anything useful; its `Create` requests don't.

  `Pages` needs a `workflowId`; publish a page before running its
  `Public Page - ...` requests (they use `pageToken`, no bearer auth).
  `Assistant`, `Dashboard`, `Library` and `Ai` only need `workspaceId`.
}
